<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The assessment lifecycle as a process.

  Plan reference: V2 sections 5.1 to 5.4, 8.2; ADR-002.

  ## What this process is, and is not

  It is the *coordination* of an assessment: who is asked to do what, in what
  order, and what happens when nobody does it in time. It is **not** the
  authority on case status. The case machine in `@tas/contracts` owns that, and
  the API is the only thing that writes `status_code`.

  That division is the whole of ADR-002. A process engine that also owned case
  state would give two systems an opinion about whether an assessment is
  approved, and they would eventually disagree. Here the engine raises work and
  waits; the API decides and tells the engine what happened.

  ## Why every user task carries roles, a step code and a form

  The API validates a definition before it reaches the engine, and refuses one
  that omits any of the three. A task with no candidate group is claimable by
  anyone; a task with no step code cannot be correlated with the transition
  table; a task with no form gives an officer nothing to do. Each of those
  leaves a case quietly stranded rather than visibly broken, which is why the
  check is a deployment gate rather than a convention.

  ## Why the service tasks call apiInvoker and nothing else

  The validator also rejects a service task naming a Java class or an arbitrary
  expression. Every outbound call goes through the one delegate, which carries
  an idempotency key derived from the process instance and activity, so a retry
  cannot double-apply anything.

  ## Literal fields and expression fields

  A `flowable:string` is a literal. A field whose value contains `${...}` has
  to be a `flowable:expression`, or the engine hands the delegate the text
  verbatim. Getting this wrong produced a request to the literal URL
  `/api/v1/cases/${caseId}/evidence/refresh` and a 500 from the HTTP client.

  ## Error boundaries

  `apiInvoker` raises `API_INVOCATION_FAILED` when the API refuses a call or
  cannot be reached. Without a boundary event catching it the error escapes the
  process, and because the first service task runs during start, a single
  unreachable source took down case creation itself. The boundary routes it to
  the task that exists to have a person resolve it.

  ## Boundary timers

  The objection window is a timer on the waiting task. It decides nothing: on
  firing it calls the API, which applies its own rules. A timer that closed a
  statutory window by itself would be a legal decision taken by a scheduler.

  The information-request window is the same shape. Asking the taxpayer for
  information interrupts preparation, the process waits, and a timer returns
  the case to the assessor when the period the taxpayer was given runs out.
  The taxpayer answering, or an officer recording an answer that arrived by
  post, cancels the timer instead.

  Neither timer carries a duration, and the information-request one is why the
  rule matters. A `PT30D` here would be the United Kingdom's response period
  written into the one definition every jurisdiction deploys, and that period
  is statutory: it is thirty days in GB and twenty business days in SA today,
  and it changes by legislation rather than by release. So the timer takes a
  date, and for this window the API supplies `infoResponseDueAt` by reading
  the `RESPONSE` deadline row it materialised from the `INFO_REQUESTED`
  anchor. The instant is the midnight that ends the last day the taxpayer may
  answer, not the midnight that begins it. The wait is then whatever that
  jurisdiction's configuration says.
-->
<definitions xmlns="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
             xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
             xmlns:flowable="http://flowable.org/bpmn"
             targetNamespace="http://iris.com/taxassessment">

  <error id="API_INVOCATION_FAILED" errorCode="API_INVOCATION_FAILED"
         name="The API refused or could not be reached" />

  <!-- Caught by name. `ProcessOrchestrationService` sends `CASE_<action>`, and
       the engine correlates by message-event subscription name, so a mismatch
       here is not an error anywhere: the message is simply never delivered. -->
  <message id="CASE_REQUEST_INFO" name="CASE_REQUEST_INFO" />
  <message id="CASE_RESPOND" name="CASE_RESPOND" />

  <process id="TAX_ASSESSMENT_MAIN" name="Tax Assessment" isExecutable="true">

    <documentation>
      Coordinates an assessment from evidence retrieval to service of the
      notice. The API owns case status; this process owns who is asked to act.
    </documentation>

    <startEvent id="start" name="Case opened" />
    <sequenceFlow id="f1" sourceRef="start" targetRef="retrieveEvidence" />

    <!-- Evidence is retrieved by the platform, not by a person: DATA_READY is
         a statement that the data arrived, not a claim anybody makes. -->
    <serviceTask id="retrieveEvidence" name="Retrieve evidence"
                 flowable:delegateExpression="${apiInvoker}">
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:field name="method">
          <flowable:string>POST</flowable:string>
        </flowable:field>
        <flowable:field name="endpoint">
          <flowable:expression>/api/v1/cases/${caseId}/evidence/refresh</flowable:expression>
        </flowable:field>
        <flowable:field name="outputVariable">
          <flowable:string>evidence</flowable:string>
        </flowable:field>
      </extensionElements>
    </serviceTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f2" sourceRef="retrieveEvidence" targetRef="evidenceGate" />

    <boundaryEvent id="evidenceFailed" attachedToRef="retrieveEvidence" cancelActivity="true">
      <errorEventDefinition errorRef="API_INVOCATION_FAILED" />
    </boundaryEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f2e" sourceRef="evidenceFailed" targetRef="chaseEvidence" />

    <!-- A failed mandatory source must not be worked around by the process. -->
    <exclusiveGateway id="evidenceGate" name="Data ready?" />
    <sequenceFlow id="f3" sourceRef="evidenceGate" targetRef="chaseEvidence">
      <conditionExpression xsi:type="tFormalExpression"><![CDATA[${evidence == null || evidence.dataReady != true}]]></conditionExpression>
    </sequenceFlow>
    <sequenceFlow id="f4" sourceRef="evidenceGate" targetRef="prepareAssessment">
      <conditionExpression xsi:type="tFormalExpression"><![CDATA[${evidence != null && evidence.dataReady == true}]]></conditionExpression>
    </sequenceFlow>

    <userTask id="chaseEvidence" name="Resolve an evidence failure"
              flowable:candidateGroups="TA_SUPERVISOR,TA_ADMIN">
      <documentation>
        A mandatory source could not be read. Somebody has to fix the source or
        decide to proceed without it; the process will not assume either.
      </documentation>
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:properties>
          <flowable:property name="stepCode" value="EVIDENCE_EXCEPTION" />
          <flowable:property name="formId" value="TA-EVIDENCE-EXCEPTION" />
          <flowable:property name="roles" value="TA_SUPERVISOR,TA_ADMIN" />
        </flowable:properties>
      </extensionElements>
    </userTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f5" sourceRef="chaseEvidence" targetRef="retrieveEvidence" />

    <userTask id="prepareAssessment" name="Prepare the assessment"
              flowable:candidateGroups="TA_ASSESSOR,TA_SPECIALIST">
      <documentation>
        Record adjustments and calculate. Completed by the officer when the
        figures are ready to submit for review.
      </documentation>
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:properties>
          <flowable:property name="stepCode" value="PREPARE" />
          <flowable:property name="formId" value="TA-04-PREPARATION" />
          <flowable:property name="roles" value="TA_ASSESSOR,TA_SPECIALIST" />
        </flowable:properties>
      </extensionElements>
    </userTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f6" sourceRef="prepareAssessment" targetRef="review" />

    <!--
      The information request.

      Interrupting, because preparation genuinely stops: the officer asked a
      question and cannot finish the assessment until it is answered. A
      non-interrupting boundary would leave the preparation task claimable
      while the case sits in AWAITING_TAXPAYER.
    -->
    <boundaryEvent id="informationRequested" attachedToRef="prepareAssessment" cancelActivity="true">
      <messageEventDefinition messageRef="CASE_REQUEST_INFO" />
    </boundaryEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f6a" sourceRef="informationRequested" targetRef="awaitInformation" />

    <!-- A receive task, like the objection window, because nobody in the
         authority is acting here. -->
    <receiveTask id="awaitInformation" name="Information request open" />
    <sequenceFlow id="f6b" sourceRef="awaitInformation" targetRef="prepareAssessment" />

    <boundaryEvent id="informationSupplied" attachedToRef="awaitInformation" cancelActivity="true">
      <messageEventDefinition messageRef="CASE_RESPOND" />
    </boundaryEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f6c" sourceRef="informationSupplied" targetRef="prepareAssessment" />

    <boundaryEvent id="informationWindowLapsed" attachedToRef="awaitInformation" cancelActivity="true">
      <timerEventDefinition>
        <timeDate>${infoResponseDueAt}</timeDate>
      </timerEventDefinition>
    </boundaryEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f6d" sourceRef="informationWindowLapsed" targetRef="timeOutRequest" />

    <serviceTask id="timeOutRequest" name="Time out the information request"
                 flowable:delegateExpression="${apiInvoker}">
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:field name="method">
          <flowable:string>POST</flowable:string>
        </flowable:field>
        <flowable:field name="endpoint">
          <flowable:expression>/api/v1/cases/${caseId}/transition</flowable:expression>
        </flowable:field>
        <flowable:field name="inputExpression">
          <flowable:string>{"action":"TIMEOUT"}</flowable:string>
        </flowable:field>
      </extensionElements>
    </serviceTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f6e" sourceRef="timeOutRequest" targetRef="prepareAssessment" />

    <!--
      The 4xx this will actually see is the API refusing TIMEOUT because the
      case has already left AWAITING_TAXPAYER, because somebody recorded the
      response in the moment the timer fired. `apiInvoker` turns that into a BpmnError,
      and without a boundary it escapes and strands the instance. The case is
      already back in preparation, so the process rejoins it.
    -->
    <boundaryEvent id="timeOutRefused" attachedToRef="timeOutRequest" cancelActivity="true">
      <errorEventDefinition errorRef="API_INVOCATION_FAILED" />
    </boundaryEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f6f" sourceRef="timeOutRefused" targetRef="prepareAssessment" />

    <userTask id="review" name="Review the assessment"
              flowable:candidateGroups="TA_REVIEWER">
      <documentation>
        The API enforces that the reviewer is not the officer who prepared the
        case. This task only decides who is asked.
      </documentation>
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:properties>
          <flowable:property name="stepCode" value="REVIEW" />
          <flowable:property name="formId" value="TA-11-REVIEW" />
          <flowable:property name="roles" value="TA_REVIEWER" />
        </flowable:properties>
      </extensionElements>
    </userTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f7" sourceRef="review" targetRef="reviewGate" />

    <exclusiveGateway id="reviewGate" name="Accepted?" />
    <sequenceFlow id="f8" sourceRef="reviewGate" targetRef="prepareAssessment">
      <conditionExpression xsi:type="tFormalExpression"><![CDATA[${reviewOutcome == 'RETURN'}]]></conditionExpression>
    </sequenceFlow>
    <sequenceFlow id="f9" sourceRef="reviewGate" targetRef="approve">
      <conditionExpression xsi:type="tFormalExpression"><![CDATA[${reviewOutcome != 'RETURN'}]]></conditionExpression>
    </sequenceFlow>

    <!--
      One approval task for all three levels.

      The delegation limits decide which level is required, and the API routes
      accordingly. Three separate tasks here would put the limits in two places
      and let them drift.
    -->
    <userTask id="approve" name="Approve the assessment"
              flowable:candidateGroups="TA_APPROVER_L1,TA_APPROVER_L2,TA_APPROVER_L3">
      <documentation>
        The required approver level comes from the amount. The API refuses an
        approval from a level the delegation limits do not permit.
      </documentation>
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:properties>
          <flowable:property name="stepCode" value="APPROVE" />
          <flowable:property name="formId" value="TA-12-APPROVAL" />
          <flowable:property name="roles" value="TA_APPROVER_L1,TA_APPROVER_L2,TA_APPROVER_L3" />
        </flowable:properties>
      </extensionElements>
    </userTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f10" sourceRef="approve" targetRef="approvalGate" />

    <exclusiveGateway id="approvalGate" name="Approved?" />
    <sequenceFlow id="f11" sourceRef="approvalGate" targetRef="prepareAssessment">
      <conditionExpression xsi:type="tFormalExpression"><![CDATA[${approvalOutcome == 'REJECT'}]]></conditionExpression>
    </sequenceFlow>
    <sequenceFlow id="f12" sourceRef="approvalGate" targetRef="issueNotice">
      <conditionExpression xsi:type="tFormalExpression"><![CDATA[${approvalOutcome != 'REJECT'}]]></conditionExpression>
    </sequenceFlow>

    <userTask id="issueNotice" name="Issue and serve the notice"
              flowable:candidateGroups="TA_NOTICE_ISSUER,TA_SUPERVISOR">
      <documentation>
        Generate the notice and serve it. Deemed service is computed by the API
        from the jurisdiction rule; the objection window runs from that date,
        not from despatch.
      </documentation>
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:properties>
          <flowable:property name="stepCode" value="NOTICE" />
          <flowable:property name="formId" value="TA-14-NOTICE" />
          <flowable:property name="roles" value="TA_NOTICE_ISSUER,TA_SUPERVISOR" />
        </flowable:properties>
      </extensionElements>
    </userTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f13" sourceRef="issueNotice" targetRef="awaitResponse" />

    <!--
      The objection window.

      A receive task rather than a user task: nobody in the authority is doing
      anything here. It ends when the taxpayer objects, or when the window
      lapses and the boundary timer fires.
    -->
    <receiveTask id="awaitResponse" name="Objection window open" />
    <sequenceFlow id="f14" sourceRef="awaitResponse" targetRef="objectionRaised" />

    <boundaryEvent id="windowLapsed" attachedToRef="awaitResponse" cancelActivity="true">
      <timerEventDefinition>
        <!-- Set from the materialised objection deadline when the API reports
             the notice served. A literal here would be a statutory date
             hard-coded into a diagram. -->
        <timeDate>${objectionWindowEndsAt}</timeDate>
      </timerEventDefinition>
    </boundaryEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="f15" sourceRef="windowLapsed" targetRef="closeWindow" />

    <serviceTask id="closeWindow" name="Close the response window"
                 flowable:delegateExpression="${apiInvoker}">
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:field name="method">
          <flowable:string>POST</flowable:string>
        </flowable:field>
        <flowable:field name="endpoint">
          <flowable:expression>/api/v1/cases/${caseId}/transition</flowable:expression>
        </flowable:field>
        <flowable:field name="inputExpression">
          <flowable:string>{"action":"WINDOW_LAPSED"}</flowable:string>
        </flowable:field>
      </extensionElements>
    </serviceTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="f16" sourceRef="closeWindow" targetRef="endUncontested" />

    <endEvent id="endUncontested" name="Uncontested" />

    <!--
      A dispute leaves this process.

      Objections and appeals have their own lifecycles, their own deadlines and
      their own officers. Modelling them as a branch here would produce one
      diagram nobody can read, which has to change whenever either lifecycle
      does.
    -->
    <endEvent id="objectionRaised" name="Objection filed" />

  </process>
</definitions>
